
alock++
Immir <mjsmi36>, Dec 2025.

This is a *heavily* modified version of alock, an X11 screen locker.
It provides password progress dots, image and shaded-screen backgrounds,
optional web backgrounds, and embedded terminal/ttyrec backgrounds.
Configuration uses an INI file; an example appears below.


Building
--------

Run from the source directory:

  make

This builds ./alock++ with PAM authentication and Xrandr multi-monitor
support. WebKit support is disabled by default. No configure step is
needed.

To build without Xrandr support:

  make clean
  make ENABLE_XRANDR=0

To enable HTML/CSS/JS backgrounds, install the WebKitGTK 4.0 and GTK+ 3
development packages, including their pkg-config metadata, then run:

  make clean
  make ENABLE_WEBKIT=1

The Makefile queries pkg-config for webkit2gtk-4.0 and gtk+-3.0. If these
packages are installed in a nonstandard prefix, set PKG_CONFIG_PATH to
its pkgconfig directory. The Makefile also retains optional search paths
under local/ for manually supplied compatibility headers and metadata;
that directory is not bundled or needed for the default build.

Run make clean before changing feature flags: make does not track changes
to compiler options. There is no install target; the executable is built
in the source directory.


Dependencies
------------

Default build:
- A C compiler and make.
- Headers and libraries for X11, Xext (including X Shape), Xrender,
  Xrandr, and PAM, plus the system math library.
- An X11 session and a working PAM service configuration at runtime.

Xrandr can be disabled with ENABLE_XRANDR=0. Image loading uses the bundled
stb_image.h; Imlib2 is no longer required.

For Linux systems with the PAM runtime but no PAM development headers,
etc/security/ contains fallback Linux-PAM headers. From the source
directory, build the default configuration with:

  make clean
  make CFLAGS='-Wall -O2 -I. -idirafter ./etc' \
       LIBS='-lX11 -lXext -lXrender -l:libpam.so.0 -lm -lXrandr'

This uses GNU ld's exact-filename syntax to link the installed PAM runtime.
See etc/README.md for details. The runtime library, PAM configuration,
and authentication modules must still be installed.

Optional:
- Web backgrounds: WebKitGTK 4.0 and GTK+ 3 headers/libraries, pkg-config,
  and their dependencies at build time; the corresponding runtime
  libraries at runtime.
- Terminal backgrounds: xterm (the default) or another terminal emulator
  with embedding support, plus the program it will run. ttyplay is needed
  only for ttyrec playback.


Usage
-----

  ./alock++                                    # Basic lock with dots
  ./alock++ -bg blank:color=black              # Black background
  ./alock++ -bg image:file=/path/to/img.png    # Image background
  ./alock++ -bg shade:shade=75                 # Shaded screen
  ./alock++ -bg web:url=file:///path/to/anim.html  # WebKit (if enabled)
  ./alock++ -bg term:cmd=top                   # Embedded terminal program
  ./alock++ -bg 'term:cmd=ttyplay game.ttyrec' # ttyrec playback "screensaver"

See ./alock++ -help for full documentation.


Terminal background (-bg term)
------------------------------

Runs a real terminal emulator (xterm by default) as the lock screen,
reparented into the lock window, running a command of your choice -- e.g.
a ttyrec playback of NetHack as a screensaver. alock grabs the keyboard on
the root window, so the embedded terminal never receives input: typing the
password still unlocks, and the terminal can't be used to escape the lock.

  Options (term:opt=val,opt=val,...):
    cmd=<command>     Program to run inside the terminal (default: top).
    cmdN=<command>    Per-monitor command; N is the monitor index in Xrandr
                      order (cmd0, cmd1, ...). Monitors without an indexed
                      command fall back to bare cmd=.
    term=<template>   Emulator + embed flags, must contain %w (the window id).
                      Default: xterm -into %w -fa Monospace -fs 18 ... -e
                      Other emulators: 'st -w %w -e', 'urxvt -embed %w -e'.
    bgcolor=<color>   Background color around the terminal (default: black).
    fill=0|1          1: resize the terminal to fill its monitor;
                      0 (default): center it at its natural size.
    respawn=0|1       1 (default): relaunch the command when it exits, so a
                      finite ttyrec loops. Rate-limited; gives up on a
                      monitor after repeated immediate exits.

  On a multi-monitor (Xrandr) setup each connected output gets its own
  terminal. Best paired with '-input dots' (the dots float transparently
  over the terminal via the X Shape extension) or '-input none'.

  Examples:
    ./alock++ -bg term                         # placeholder: xterm + top
    ./alock++ -bg 'term:cmd=ttyplay g.ttyrec'  # same playback everywhere
    ./alock++ -bg 'term:cmd0=ttyplay a.ttyrec,cmd1=ttyplay b.ttyrec'
    ./alock++ -bg 'term:term=st -w %w -e,cmd=top,fill=1'

  The ttyrec playback tool (e.g. ttyplay) is supplied separately; any
  program that writes to the terminal works (top, cmatrix, etc.).

  Config file (~/.alockrc) form:
    [background]
    module = term
    cmd = ttyplay /path/to/nethack.ttyrec
    fill = 1


Configuration
-------------

The configuration file is $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/alock/config when that file
exists, otherwise ~/.alockrc. Command-line module selections override
those in the file.

Example ~/.alockrc:

  [auth]
  module = pam

  [background]
  module = blank
  color = black

  [input]
  module = dots
  dot_placement = center_left
  dot_radius = 5
  dot_spacing = 10
  dot_color = white
  success_color = green
  error_color = red

Use ./alock++ -modules to list the modules compiled into your build.
Image loading uses stb_image.h, despite the older Imlib2 wording still
present in ./alock++ -help.
